# 0078-no-shader-library-is-a-failed-build.patch -- a build (or a Play) that leaves the game with NO shader library, or a build that leaves NO framework (the assembly or the signature
# failed), is shown as what it is, a failed run with its reason, never "Ready" or "Built: ready to play." (review r4 §4.1 / §2.2: the chain reported `shaders=failed (no library: ...)`,
# returned 0, and the app said "Ready" while the game would draw nothing; the same 0 for `assemble=failed`, with "Built: ready to play." for a game that is not there).
# Applies after 0077. UNTESTED ON DEVICE AND IN THE SIMULATOR: compiled only, and its pure part ran in a host check (below).
#
# THE CONTRACT (crates/ibuilder-ffi/include/ibuilder.h, docs/chain/PIPELINE-RUST.md "Result codes"; decided in the Rust chain, the shim passes the code through unchanged and pins 4 and 5)
#   * gameapp_run_chain() -> 4 (IBUILDER_RC_NO_SHADER_LIBRARY): the game has no shader library and would draw nothing.
#       - a build: stage 3 had to make the library and could not (too little space, no translator, inputs missing, the split, the translation or the install failed). The chain still went on
#         to the assembly (as before, as in Python), but it did NOT boot the game, did NOT write gameapp.boot-only, and wrote NO framework stamp (Documents/gameapp.framework): the existing
#         rule (GameAppUi.ClassifyFramework / GameStatusOf) reads that framework as not built: "Needs setup", "Set up and play", never "Ready".
#       - a Play (boot-only): its stage 3 could not make a missing library: the boot is refused and the request consumed (a later launch does not start it by itself); the framework stays
#         this build's, so the game stays Ready and a later Play tries the library again.
#   * gameapp_run_chain() -> 5 (IBUILDER_RC_NO_FRAMEWORK): no framework to play: stage 4 failed, the assembly or the signature (the signer's rc 10-14: a missing or unreadable certificate,
#     ...). Nothing was stamped or booted (as before); it returned 0, as Python did. When stage 3 failed too, the chain returns 5 (the log says both).
#   * `shaders=none (...)` (nothing to build: no chain in the config, shader_build=0, a game whose split finds no shader) keeps 0. Every other code keeps its meaning (0 1 2 3 75 130 131).
#   * Without this patch the app already treats 4 and 5 as failed runs (StopMachine.ChainReturned: anything but 0, 130, 131 is Failed): the failure is not hidden, only worded generically
#     ("Stopped at <the step it ended on>", which is the assembly's step). This patch gives them their words.
#
# WHAT IT DOES
#   * GameAppStop.cs (no UIKit): ChainResult: NoShaderLibrary = 4, NoFramework = 5 and their texts: FailureText (the message), RunLine, QueueNote, LogLine ("[RED] " + the message: an
#     error row); null for every other code (the generic texts stay).
#   * GameAppMain.cs, BeginRun: lastChainRc = 0 when a run starts; when the chain returns, lastChainRc = rc (-1 when it threw), and a failed run whose code has words writes its LogLine
#     right away, before Finish() draws the screen: it is the build screen's last error (its live rows) and where "See what stopped it" scrolls.
#   * GameAppLibrary.cs: the run line under the game says "Shader library not built · <time>" / "Not assembled or signed · <time>" instead of "Stopped at <step>"; the "Notify me"
#     notification says the message instead of "The build stopped: open iBuilder to see why."; the field lastChainRc.
#   * GameAppQueueUi.cs: the build queue's note for that game is the code's note.
#
# NEW UI STRINGS (worth a marker in app/verify_ipa.sh, owned elsewhere)
#   "The shader library could not be built: the game would draw nothing. Rebuild, or send the log."   (ChainResult.NoShaderLibraryText)
#   "Shader library not built"                                                                         (ChainResult.NoShaderLibraryLine)
#   "the shader library could not be built: see the log"                                               (ChainResult.NoShaderLibraryNote)
#   "The game could not be assembled or signed: see the log."                                          (ChainResult.NoFrameworkText)
#   "Not assembled or signed"                                                                          (ChainResult.NoFrameworkLine)
#   "the game could not be assembled or signed: see the log"                                           (ChainResult.NoFrameworkNote)
#
# VERIFIED (2026-11-21, M1 Pro; the scratch of the first checks was wiped by a reboot, everything below was run again after it)
#   * Compile: the app project generated as scripts/build-app.sh step 3 makes it (the patched GameApp*.cs, the engine's extractor and translator sources read from the read-only engine, the
#     same csproj items), built with `dotnet build gameapp.csproj -c Release -r ios-arm64 -t:Compile`, the native references left out (the C# compile alone, no link; no .ipa): the tree with
#     0077 rc 0, 0 errors, 40 warnings; with this patch rc 0, 0 errors, 40 warnings, the same warnings per file and code. The six texts above are in the compiled GameApp.dll (UTF-16).
#     (Before the reboot, with 4 only: a full build of the earlier device project, 0 errors, 41 warnings with and without the patch, the same per file and code.)
#   * Host harness (net11.0; the REAL GameAppStop.cs, GameAppNas.cs with NAS_LOGIC_ONLY, GameAppCloud.cs, GameAppUi.cs, GameAppCover.cs, GameAppQueue.cs, GameAppPerf.cs; the Rust and C texts
#     read for what they share): 61 of 61 checks. For this patch: 4 and 5 are ibuilder.h's IBUILDER_RC_NO_SHADER_LIBRARY / IBUILDER_RC_NO_FRAMEWORK and the Rust RC_NO_SHADER_LIBRARY /
#     RC_NO_FRAMEWORK, the header's nine codes are distinct; StopMachine: 4 and 5 are Failed (4 from a boot-only Play too), 0 Finished, 1 and 3 Failed, 131 Paused, 130 Cancelled; the texts of
#     4 and of 5, none for 0 1 2 3 6 75 130 131 -1 -99; their log lines are error rows (GameAppUi.KindOfLine), and so are the chain's own "stage 5 NOT STARTED: [RED]" lines (no library,
#     not assembled or signed) and "stage 5 boot-only REFUSED: [RED]" (read from chain.rs / boot.rs); a framework with no stamp is Needs setup with the Run action, a stamped one Ready
#     (control), and assemble.rs stamps only when stage 3 did not fail; the wiring read from the UIKit files (reset at the start, kept and said before Finish(), the run line, the
#     notification, the queue). 14 of 14 mutations of this patch (and of the Rust / C text it reads) are killed (code 5 for 4, code 6 for 5, no [RED], words for every failure, no words for 5,
#     5 with 4's run line, the line not written, not reset, not kept, queue / notification not wired, the stamp written whatever stage 3 did, the header's codes moved).
#   * The Rust side (cargo test -p ibuilder-pipeline -p ibuilder-ffi -p ibuilder-cli): a build whose stage 3 fails returns 4, boots nothing, writes no boot-only request and no stamp; a
#     boot-only launch whose stage 3 fails refuses the boot, returns 4 and consumes the request; a build whose signature fails returns 5 (and 5 when stage 3 failed too), boots nothing;
#     `none` keeps 0 and boots; the C ABI test sees 4, 5 and their status through the real header. check-patches-apply.sh: 34 patches, no fuzz, no offset.
#
# NOT VERIFIED
#   * Nothing ran on an iPhone or in the simulator: the screen with these messages, the notification and the queue row are not looked at.
#   * A build that fails at stage 3 or 4 in the app (a real translator failing, a full disk, a missing certificate) was not reproduced: the Rust tests reach the same code through stand-in
#     workers (a failing translation, a huge shader_free_gib, a signer that returns 12).
